<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!--
	    Without this, every system command and every flow step that touches the Mac is dead in a
	    signed build. Hardened runtime blocks Apple Events unless the app declares it, and the block
	    happens *before* macOS asks the person anything: no prompt appears, and BeLauncher never
	    shows up in System Settings › Privacy › Automation, so there is nothing to switch on either.
	    The flow reports success and changes nothing.

	    It went unnoticed because the development build is not signed with hardened runtime, so it
	    worked on the machine where it was tested and only on that path.

	    This is the only entitlement here on purpose. It permits sending events; it does not grant
	    access to any particular app — macOS still asks per target the first time.
	-->
	<key>com.apple.security.automation.apple-events</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
